  So the two halves of this repo don't talk to each other, and there's no real session concept yet — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m doesn't
  establish one.

  [1mThe first fork: where preferences actually live.[0m

  This determines the data model, the API surface, and what "across sessions" can even mean, and it's expensive to
  change later — the read/write interface the rest of the UI codes against differs between the options.

  - [1mBrowser localStorage[0m — preferences persist on one device, survive tab close and reload, no backend needed. Costs
    nothing to build and works today since the login is a stub. Rules out: same settings on a second device, and
    anything server-rendered.
  - [1mServer-backed via the API[0m — preferences follow the user everywhere and survive clearing browser data. But [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is
    a stub with no real auth, no session token, and no server in this repo, so this pulls a backend (or at least a mock
    one) into scope alongside the preferences work.
  - [1mlocalStorage now, behind a storage interface[0m — same cheap build, but reads/writes go through a small
    [38;5;153mPreferencesStore[39m seam so a server-backed implementation can be dropped in later without touching call sites.
    Slightly more structure up front.

  My recommendation is the third: this app has a login form, so per-user server-side settings are a plausible future,
  but building a backend now means building auth first. The seam costs maybe a dozen extra lines and keeps that door
  open.
